 5 Hurricanes Hurricanes are a consistent coastal threat that is increasingly exacerbated by climate change… 5.1 Environmental Data Retreival Hurricane data was sourced from the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA). Data can be obtained programmatically (as below), or manually for each hurricane of interest from the NOAA website. Should you choose to download the files yourself, the script is designed to use shapefiles (.shp). It is important to note that the following chunks should be run sparingly to avoid overloading the target. We first need to specify when and where the hurricane data is retrieved from. base_page &lt;- &quot;https://www.nhc.noaa.gov/gis/archive_besttrack.php?year=&quot; base_zip &lt;- &quot;https://www.nhc.noaa.gov/gis/best_track/&quot; hurricane_years_interest &lt;- 2008:2025 #getting data for all years from 2008 to 2025 Next, we set up the storage location for the hurricane best track files and create a lookup table for later. It is important to set a viable output directory for the best tracks from the website. #set an output directory for the data to download into base_shape_dir &lt;- &quot;./data/HurricaneRawData/nhc_best_tracks&quot; dir.create(base_shape_dir, showWarnings = FALSE) #create a lookup table for the storms that can be used later to create a connection between the storm name and data storm_summary &lt;- data.frame(Year = integer(), Storms = integer()) storm_lookup &lt;- data.frame(year = integer(), storm_id = character(), storm_name = character()) Finally, we get the files from the website for each year specified. Within this snippet, a look-up table for the file names and actual hurricane name is created and saved as a .csv for later reference. #process every year for the year range specified above for (year in hurricane_years_interest) { cat(&quot;processing&quot;, year, &quot;
